<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-health/foundational/profession-and-science-of-public-health">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1920 C.-E. A. Winslow defined public health as <strong>"the science and art of
    preventing disease, prolonging life and promoting health through the organised efforts
    of society"</strong> — and every word earns its place. <em>Preventing</em>: the field's
    centre of gravity is upstream of illness, not beside the sickbed. <em>Organised efforts
    of society</em>: not the doctor's art of curing one patient, but laws, sewers,
    inspections, campaigns and data systems — the machinery of whole communities. That is the
    first foundational knowledge area of every CEPH-accredited programme: <em>explain public
    health history, philosophy, and values; identify the core functions of public health and
    the 10 Essential Services</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The philosophical shift from medicine is worth making vivid. A physician asks: what is
    wrong with <em>this patient</em>, and what will fix them? A public-health professional
    asks: why is <em>this population</em> sick, and what would make it healthier? The second
    question has answers the first cannot see. When a doctor treats a child's asthma, the
    case is treated; when public health finds the mould in the housing estate and forces the
    landlord to fix it, the next hundred cases never occur. Geoffrey Rose, the field's great
    modern theorist, called this the distinction between the <em>sick individual</em> and the
    <em>sick population</em>: the causes of cases and the causes of incidence are different
    things, and only the second changes how many people get sick at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field's <strong>values</strong> follow from the population perspective. Health is a
    public good: it depends on shared environments (air, water, food, housing), so improving
    it requires collective action — and its benefits (herd immunity, clean air, safer roads)
    are mostly invisible precisely because they are the diseases that never happened. Two
    commitments come with that: <strong>prevention over cure</strong> wherever prevention is
    possible, and <strong>equity</strong> — because health outcomes are distributed
    unfairly, and a population average that hides a preventable gap is, on the field's own
    definition, a failure. History, philosophy, values: they are one story, told below.
  </p>

  <h2>A profession built by counting</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="from one doctor's map to global eradication — the milestones that built the profession" widget="timeline" config={phTimeline} />
  <p>
    The pattern in the timeline is worth naming, because it is the philosophy: public health
    advances when <em>evidence</em> precedes action. Jenner and Snow acted on observation;
    WHO institutionalised cooperation; smallpox proved the model end to end. Between Snow and
    WHO sit the other load-bearing moments: Edwin Chadwick's 1842 report on the sanitary
    condition of the labouring population tied filth to fever and produced the 1848 Public
    Health Act — the state's first systematic entry into sewerage and water; John Graunt's
    <em>Natural and Political Observations upon the Bills of Mortality</em> (1662) had earlier
    shown that deaths could be counted and patterns extracted, inventing vital statistics;
    Florence Nightingale's polar-area diagrams of Crimean War deaths (1858) made statistics
    into an argument generals and ministers could not ignore. Each step is the same move:
    count, compare, conclude, intervene.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> public health is not "medicine for poor countries" or
  "healthcare without the hospital". It is a distinct discipline with its own methods and its
  own object of study (the population, not the patient) — and its interventions (fluoridated
  water, seatbelt laws, tobacco taxes, vaccination programmes) mostly operate on everyone at
  once, including the healthy and the wealthy.</p>

  <h2>Core functions and the 10 Essential Services</h2>
  <p>
    The 1988 Institute of Medicine report <em>The Future of Public Health</em> fixed three
    <strong>core functions</strong>: <em>assessment</em> (monitor and measure what a
    population's health actually is), <em>policy development</em> (turn evidence into
    decisions), and <em>assurance</em> (guarantee the services get delivered). The US
    <strong>10 Essential Public Health Services</strong> (updated 2020) translate those
    functions into an operating checklist:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>assess and monitor population health; investigate, diagnose and address health hazards and root causes</li>
    <li>communicate effectively to inform and educate; strengthen, support and empower communities</li>
    <li>create, champion and implement policies, plans and laws; enforce laws and regulations that protect health</li>
    <li>assure equitable access to care; build a strong, diverse, skilled public-health workforce and infrastructure</li>
    <li>improve and innovate through ongoing evaluation, research and continuous quality improvement</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notice what the list is <em>not</em>: it is not a hospital system. Clinical care for
    individuals appears once, inside "assure access" — the rest is population work. Notice
    also how the functions pair up: assessment is the counting half, policy development the
    deciding half, assurance the delivering half — and the equity commitment is written
    directly into the list ("health equity" is the framework's stated centre).
  </p>
  <p>
    The instrument kit that makes this work is CEPH's objective 3: quantitative and
    qualitative methods for describing and assessing a population's health. Epidemiology
    and biostatistics count who gets sick, where, and of what — the surveillance that made
    Snow's map possible and that cornered smallpox — while qualitative methods capture the
    beliefs and circumstances behind the numbers. Objective 4 adds the discipline's
    honesty clause: morbidity and mortality are always broken down by population, because
    averages hide disparities. A city's life expectancy is a different number in its
    richest and poorest wards — gaps of a decade or more within single cities are common —
    and acting on <em>that</em> difference is what the profession's values actually commit
    it to.
  </p>

  <h2>Major causes and trends of morbidity and mortality</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mortality</strong> is death in a population; <strong>morbidity</strong> is
    illness, injury and disability. The first thing the numbers teach is that populations
    die of different things at different stages of development — the <strong>epidemiologic
    transition</strong>. In 1850s England, the great killers were infectious: tuberculosis,
    cholera, typhoid, diphtheria, and infections carried off roughly half of all children
    before their fifth birthday (life expectancy "at birth" of 40 is dragged down mostly by
    child mortality, not by adults dying young). As sanitation, nutrition and then vaccines
    and antibiotics took hold, infectious disease receded and <strong>chronic, non-
    communicable disease</strong> rose to dominance: in high-income countries today,
    cardiovascular disease, cancers, chronic respiratory disease and diabetes account for
    the large majority of deaths, with dementia climbing as longevity increases. The
    transition's drivers are exactly the field's levers — environment, behaviour, and the
    organised services that deliver them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two measurement tools turn those facts into policy. <em>Life expectancy at birth</em>
    summarises mortality across ages in one number — the curve below. <em>DALYs</em>
    (disability-adjusted life years — years of healthy life lost to premature death plus
    years lived with disability) combine mortality and morbidity so that a condition that
    cripples without killing is still counted, which reshapes the priority list: depression
    and musculoskeletal disorders, nearly invisible in death statistics, sit near the top of
    the global burden-of-disease tables. And the honesty clause applies throughout: the same
    country's data disaggregated by income, race, region or sex shows gaps that the headline
    numbers conceal — in the US, for example, maternal mortality is more than twice as high
    for Black women as for white women, a disparity that is an output of social conditions,
    not biology.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "life expectancy was 40, so people dropped dead at 40."
  No — the average was 40 because so many died as infants and children. Someone surviving
  childhood in 1850 had a decent chance of reaching 60 or 70. Reading population statistics
  correctly (which average, which denominator, which gap) is itself a public-health skill,
  and the misreading is how people conclude that vaccines "did nothing" for mortality.</p>

  <h2>The prevention pyramid</h2>
  <p>
    CEPH objective 5 asks you to "discuss the science of primary, secondary and tertiary
    prevention". Public health arranges intervention in three tiers:
    <strong>primary prevention</strong> stops disease before it starts — vaccination, clean
    water, seatbelts, clean-air laws, tobacco taxation; <strong>secondary prevention</strong>
    catches disease early, before symptoms or damage — screening for cervical cancer,
    blood-pressure checks, newborn screening; <strong>tertiary prevention</strong> limits
    the damage once disease exists — rehabilitation after stroke, diabetic complication
    management, cardiac rehab. Worked example with real arithmetic: smoking. Primary
    prevention (preventing uptake, raising prices, smoke-free laws) averts cases that never
    need treating; a pack of cigarettes taxed by half can cut consumption measurably across
    a whole population, and adult smoking prevalence in the US and UK fell from around 40%
    in the 1960s to under 15% today — one of the largest single health gains of the century,
    achieved mostly outside clinics. Secondary prevention (lung screening for long-term
    smokers) finds tumours while surgery is still curative; tertiary prevention (pulmonary
    rehabilitation) restores function for those already sick. The pyramid's logic is
    economic as well as moral: the higher up you act, the more people you touch per pound —
    and the less suffering you have to undo. The pyramid also explains the field's hardest
    politics: primary prevention regulates the healthy for the sake of the not-yet-sick,
    which is why seatbelt laws and sugar taxes generate controversies that no treatment
    programme ever does.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence: the discipline's spine</h2>
  <p>
    The importance of evidence in public health (CEPH objective 6) is not an abstract value;
    it is literally how the profession was assembled, and it has a method that can be
    specified. Epidemiology's basic designs form an evidence ladder: <em>case reports</em>
    and <em>cross-sectional</em> surveys describe; <em>cohort studies</em> follow exposed and
    unexposed groups forward (the British Doctors Study, launched 1951, tracked some 34,000
    male doctors for fifty years and established smoking as a cause of lung cancer);
    <em>case-control studies</em> compare the pasts of the sick and the well; and
    <em>randomised controlled trials</em> — used for vaccines and treatments — allocate
    exposure by chance, the cleanest test of cause. The <strong>Bradford Hill
    considerations</strong> (1965) supply the checklist for causal claims in the messy
    observational world public health actually inhabits: strength of association, dose–
    response, consistency across studies, biological plausibility, temporality, and more.
    Evidence then has to survive translation into policy — which is where value judgements
    enter (how much risk is acceptable? whose freedoms?) and why communication, community
    engagement and transparency are Essential Services and not decorations. COVID-19 ran the
    whole apparatus at speed and in public: surveillance first, then trials in months, then
    the arguments — over masks, schools, mandates — that showed the science-evidence-policy
    seam in real time.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The dividend, stated quantitatively: the curve (approximate values from the Our World in
    Data / Human Mortality Database series) roughly doubles in 170 years — about 40 years in
    1850, about 47 by 1900, 68 by 1950, 77 by 2000, 79 by 2020 in England and Wales. The
    classic finding (McKeown's demographic analysis, refined by later historians) is that
    most of the early gains came <em>before</em> modern clinical medicine: better nutrition,
    sanitation and living standards did more for mortality than the doctor's bag, and the
    sanitarians' sewers did more than most surgeries. That is the profession's core claim,
    stated in data: the organised efforts of society, measured at the population level,
    added decades to human life.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three open questions define the field's current frontier. First, the
    <strong>mortality plateau</strong>: gains in life expectancy have slowed sharply in the
    US and UK since about 2010, stalled by obesity-related disease, opioids, and (pre-
    pandemic) widening socioeconomic gaps — a warning that the twentieth century's levers
    are exhausted and the next ones are behavioural and structural. Second,
    <strong>health in all policies</strong>: since most of what makes populations healthy
    (housing, wages, transport, food systems) is decided outside health departments, the
    field increasingly works by advocacy across government — with all the political friction
    that implies. Third, the <strong>infodemic</strong>: misinformation now spreads faster
    than the pathogens it undermines vaccination against, making risk communication — the
    least glamorous Essential Service — suddenly the difference between a contained outbreak
    and a pandemic. Winslow's "organised efforts of society" turns out to be the whole
    job description, and it has never been more literal.
  </p>

